<h2>Start with three questions</h2>
<p>Before you choose a format, answer three things. How many players are you — two, three or four? How big is the handicap spread in the group? And do you want a relaxed social round or a hard-fought contest hole by hole?</p>
<p>The number of players matters most. Some formats need an exact number: match play is built for two, Wolf and Split Sixes for three, the team formats for four. Others, such as Stableford and skins, work for anything between two and four. The spread in standard decides whether you need handicaps or can play off gross. If the spread is wide, the choice leans towards points formats that level things out via stroke index.</p>

